{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh.</b> The Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: the Holy One, blessed be He, said two things to Moses — the first, that he should go down to deliver them from the hand of Egypt, and it was possible to deliver them from their hand in the land of Goshen itself or nearby; and the second, that he should bring them up from that land to the place of the Canaanite." "<b>And behold</b> Moses feared both of these and said: Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh — I am a lowly man, a shepherd of sheep, and he is a great king, and if I say to him to let the people go he will kill me, in the manner that Samuel said (I Samuel 16:2): ״ושמע שאול והרגני״. And further: Who am I, that I should bring the children of Israel — a wise and understanding people, this great nation — out of the land of Egypt, for they will not regard me as fit to go before great and mighty nations, and they will surely want to go out from under that great bondage, for what man is there who would not want to go out from hard labor than which there is none worse; but the ascent to the land of the Canaanite they will not accept. And so indeed it was, for the war of the Canaanites was hard upon Israel in the wilderness, and from then on they were continually afraid of it, and this was the fear of Moses. And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him on both counts: He said to him, כי אהיה עמך — for I will be with you, and do not fear Pharaoh; and וזה לך האות — and this is the sign for you for the people to whom I have sent you: כי בהוציאך את העם ממצרים תעבדון את האלהים על ההר הזה — for when you bring the people out of Egypt, you shall serve Elohim on this mountain. And from then on they would accept the service of the Holy One, blessed be He, to go after His commandments, and therefore I revealed Myself to you on this mountain in a flame of fire, for so I will always be before the eyes of all the people when they serve Me on this mountain. And behold, for Moses our teacher there was a sign in what he saw from within the bush — that he should not fear Pharaoh, since He promised to deliver him; and for Israel too there was a sign, that they should not fear the nations when they came to Mount Sinai, for they would listen to him to go out to a nearby place, a journey of three days. Thus far the language of the Rav, of blessed memory."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "The closing abbreviation ע\"כ לשון הרב ז\"ל ('thus far the language of the Rav, of blessed memory') confirms that the entirety of comment [1], including the opening <b>והנה</b> lemma, is presented as Ramban's words as introduced in comment [0]."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:52.145342996Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}